Committed as a547b058. Here's what landed and what I verified.

## The change

`src/format/cost.rs` — two conditions changed, no arm reordered:

• The shared arm if model.contains("deepseek-v4-flash") || model.contains("deepseek-r1") became the `deepseek-r1` arm alone, carrying a comment stating its 0.55/2.19 is deliberately left unchanged because it is unverifiable (deepseek-r1 is absent from models.dev's deepseek provider, so there is no second reading to check it against — not because it was re-checked).
• The already-correct deepseek-flash arm was widened with || model.contains("deepseek-v4-flash"). Its comment now records the alias, the models.dev reading (2026-09-20: deepseek-flash, deepseek-v4-flash, deepseek-v4-flash-vision-exp all {0.15, 0.6, 0.003}), the 3.7x/3.65x overstatement that made this a bug, the ordering note, and the v4-pro/v3 residue as a named non-fix rather than a silent omission.

Tests — four sites: the near-miss guard's deepseek-v4-flash row moved to (0.15, 0.0, 0.003, 0.60); model_pricing("deepseek-r1") was not touched and is now the guard that the split didn't drag r1 along; test_pricing_deepseek_v4_flash moved and gained the cache_read assertion; test_estimate_cost_deepseek_reasoner was repointed at the id it is named for ("deepseek-r1", expected 2.74 unchanged).

Docs — new V4.1-Flash row in cost-tracking.md plus the off-peak and no-drift-detection caveats; a dated Day-204 entry in ARCHITECTURE.md; register pasted 2869 → 2931 in the same diff.

## Verification

cargo build clean · cargo test 5956 passed / 0 failed · cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings clean · cargo fmt -- --check clean · module_size 28/28 with file and register both at 2931 · neutered_guards 9/9, zero markers in the tree.

Positive control run, and it found a real hole — worth flagging rather than burying. The first mutate (reverting the widened condition) reddened exactly the two intended tests by name, deepseek-r1 pins green. But it also showed my new assert_ne! stayed green under it: comparing the Options, None != Some(2.74), so it was satisfied by the alias being unpriced — a different failure than the one it names. I unwrapped both sides with expect messages, then ran a second, narrower control reproducing the original bug (r1 arm re-absorbing v4-flash, literal pin disabled), which reddens with the receipt left: 2.74, right: 2.74 ... must not share a price. So the property assertion is a real guard, and that two-step is recorded in ARCHITECTURE.md rather than the green alone.

Stated limits: only deepseek-v4-flash's row is corrected; deepseek-r1, deepseek-v3, deepseek-v4-pro keep unverified numbers; peak pricing unmodelled, so a correct row is still a lower bound. #937 stays open — this is the literal fix, task_02 is the alarm. I did not run the optional live `yoyo -p` sanity step, since it spends API budget for a number the tests already pin.
